    <strong>Is astronomy a good career in India? It is a good subject and a risky job plan: you can get a funded PhD, but every later stage is a separate selection, and permanent research posts are few.</strong>
    The people who do well treat astronomy as one strong skill, not the whole plan. They add data, software or instrument skill, show it in public, and keep more than one door open. That is the kind of <strong>high-value skill portfolio</strong> that helps you make the decisions that unlock high income opportunities, and it brings earlier financial freedom closer than a title does.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Verdict:</strong> good to study if the questions pull you, weak as a single job plan. Run a data or software skill beside it from the first year.</li>
      <li>The path is a funnel: degree, PhD gate, funded PhD, postdoc, permanent post. The Astronomical Society of India has close to 1,000 members in all, and US data shows two-thirds of new PhDs take a postdoc.</li>
      <li>Lead with the routes that can grow: data work first, then instruments, teaching and astro-tourism. Planetarium posts, ISRO-adjacent roles and the research ladder stay in, marked Not so scalable.</li>
      <li><strong>Next step:</strong> finish one small analysis of open sky-survey data and show it to three people who do the work. Avoid a loan for the degree.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

