    A 2025 paper by Indian astronomers on computational astronomy says the high-performance computing, data and machine-learning skills built in astronomy are in demand across the sciences and beyond. The authors also say the field needs more training in these areas. That is good news for you. It means you can learn them inside your astronomy work and use them anywhere.
  </p>

  <h2 id="outreach">Planetariums, outreach, dark skies and astrophotography</h2>

  <p>
    This side of astronomy is real work, but it is smaller than people expect, and it asks for different skills from research.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Posts you can apply for</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Planetarium directors and science educators, advertised one at a time by bodies such as the Prime Ministers' Museum and Library in Delhi.</li>
        <li>Science centre educators and exhibit staff under national and state science bodies.</li>
        <li>Most notices ask for a physics or astronomy master's, and some ask for years of planetarium experience.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Work you can build yourself</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Paid sky workshops for schools and colleges, and night-sky events for groups.</li>
        <li>Astrophotography prints, courses and guided shoots.</li>
        <li>Astro-tourism near a dark site. Hanle's local ambassadors were trained and given telescopes so they could earn from guiding.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Sources: planetarium notices as reported on the Prime Ministers' Museum and Library vacancy pages and job-listing sites (confirm each on the official notice); the ETV Bharat report of May 2025 on Hanle. The report gives tourist and trainee numbers but no earnings.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      Do not choose this side for the money first. Choose it if you like explaining and organising. The ownership path here is a small workshop or tour business that you run yourself. It scales because you set the price, can train others to run groups, and earn again from repeat schools and referrals. Start close to home, and track one number: enquiries that become bookings.
    </p>
  </div>

