            <td data-label="Where you still win">{item.edge}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <ol class="rich-list">
    <li><strong>Now:</strong> use AI for first drafts of code and reading notes, and test each draft on a case where you already know the answer.</li>
    <li><strong>Next:</strong> pick one chore in your project, such as reducing a night of data, and turn it into a script with a written list of ways it can fail.</li>
    <li><strong>Later:</strong> as classifiers improve, the scarce skill becomes judging them: knowing when a model trained on one survey fails on another. Start practising that now.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    We promise no fixed gain. AI helps most when you can already tell a right answer from a wrong one. Reported figures for Rubin's nightly output have varied between sources, so read the observatory's own numbers page for the current values.
  </p>

