  </p>

  <h2 id="money">Fees, loans and the education budget</h2>

  <p>
    The degree is a small part of success in this field. Skill with code and statistics, clear writing and proof you can show carry more weight. NPTEL, YouTube lectures and open archives give you most of the content for free, so a paid course must earn its fee with feedback and a finished project, not a certificate.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Where public money does the job</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Public seats reached through national tests, such as JAM, JEST, NET and GATE.</li>
        <li>The PhD places read on this page, which pay a fellowship during the degree.</li>
        <li>Olympiads and open online lectures, which cost little.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Where to be careful</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Use about 10 percent of the family's total education budget as the starting cap for college fees. If the full budget is ₹6 lakh, that is about ₹60,000, and the rest stays for living costs, a laptop, books, projects and later learning.</li>
        <li>Above roughly 20 percent, ask for course-specific proof: a required credential, current teaching and verified outcomes.</li>
        <li>Avoid education debt unless a named door requires that exact degree. Both numbers are planning rules, not laws.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="skip">Who should skip it, and the better adjacent options</h2>

